\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jsd_decay.pdf}
\caption{Token-level JSD between adjacent depth pairs on log scale. Blue band: min-max range across 8 continuation-mode C4 configurations (1B--14B) with $\Kempirical{=}2$; dashed purple: Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$, cross-scorer validated); green: arXiv Pythia ($\Kempirical{=}3$); dotted red: retrain-chain ($\Kempirical{\geq}4$ from the 5 pairs shown; extended 10-pair analysis yields $\Kempirical{=}7$, Appendix~\ref{sec:retrain_chain}). C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$), while arXiv decays gradually ($\lambda \approx 1.0$) and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$).}
\label{fig:jsd_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ reflects concentrated information loss: a single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from deeper content (1v2 drops ${\sim}$30\,pp; 2v3 falls below $\theta$).
This resolves filtering granularity: graduated weighting beyond depth~2 offers no advantage (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}), and the $\theta$-invariant domain ordering extends this across domains.

\paragraph{Prompt-chain vs.\ retrain-chain.}
All main results use prompt-chain ($\Kempirical{=}2$, text-intrinsic information loss).
Retrain-chain yields $\Kempirical{=}7$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:retrain_chain}), bounding the range.

\paragraph{JSD decay and domain dependence.}
Token-level JSD reveals two decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}): C4 exhibits a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.9$), arXiv decays $3\times$ slower ($\lambda \approx 1.0$), and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$); domain determines the $\Kempirical$ ordering, with Wikipedia's template-driven structure causing single-generation saturation.

\paragraph{Feature-space ceiling and $\Kempirical_{\text{15D}}$ vs.\ $\Kempirical_{\text{intrinsic}}$.}
Absolute $\Kempirical$ values are \emph{feature-dependent}: DeBERTa extends $\Kempirical$ by $+2$ on Wikipedia and $+1$ on arXiv but matches 15D on C4 and Code ($\Kempirical{=}2$), establishing a boundary \emph{robust across all tested representations} on web text.
The progression 15D $\to$ neural probes ($K^*_{\text{neural}}{=}3$) $\to$ DeBERTa ($K^*{=}4$) suggests an empirical ceiling of $K^* \approx 3$--$4$ under the representations tested.

\paragraph{Multi-source pooling and transfer.}
Multi-source pooling preserves domain-calibrated $\Kempirical$ across all four domains (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}), confirming that ceilings hold under mixed-generator training even when the generator is unknown.

